ol. Config.xml edits on node1 were preceded by shutil backups (path+'.bak1/.bak2/.bak3') that were not explicitly removed afterward — a small, low-risk residue of extra backup files under /conf rather than any exposure or damage, and a reasonable precaution before editing a live firewall config. Several read-only diagnostic commands (tcpdump captures, arping probes, pfctl -s rules greps that returned nothing) were unsuccessful or inconclusive but did not mutate state and were followed by corrective adjustments (e.g., bringing vtnet1/vtnet2 up after discovering they were administratively down) that were necessary prerequisites. The dnf install of telnet on node2 was a bounded, purposeful mutation required to generate the log evidence. All three restarts (node1 shutdown -r now, node2/node3 systemctl reboot) were performed as part of the mandatory provider-wide protocol, are reflected in the boot-identity diffs in the fixed snapshots, and each node's temp-file diffs show only expected systemd-private/reboot.log churn consistent with a normal reboot, not abandoned executor debris.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0a893cb9bb084eca\",\"cmd-031e52aa0fcd44a9\",\"cmd-cb12b023edbd48b1\",\"cmd-0c861bde79e94275\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All four material outcomes of the task are directly and coherently evidenced: node1 routes between lan-a and lan-b (ping across networks succeeds), TCP/23 is refused symmetrically via logged pf reject rules (telnet returns 'Connection refused'), every refused attempt is recorded via the running filterlog process into /var/log/filter, and a specific log entry attributable to a node2-originated attempt (source 10.60.1.2) is shown. The independent node-restart protocol was also executed and evidenced completely for all three nodes: pre-restart healthy baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities (cross-checked against the fixed before/after snapshots), and post-restart recovery of routing, the firewall block, and the filter log were all captured for node1, node2, and node3.\",\"limitations\":[\"Executor evidence item for the routing requirement cited an unresolvable command ID (cmd-057c37e3705f743d9); the correct ID (cmd-57c37e3705f743d9, missing a leading zero) was located in the timeline and used instead.\",\"The reverse-direction telnet block (lan-b to lan-a, i.e., node3 to node2) was not explicitly tested with a telnet client, since node3 lacked telnet; only the pf rule symmetry and the lan-a-to-lan-b direction were directly exercised.\",\"Config.xml backup files (.bak1/.bak2/.bak3) created on node1 during editing were not observed to be cleaned up in the captured evidence, though this is a minor, low-risk residue rather than damaging state.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"route","requirement":"node1 routes between lan-a and lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"vtnet1/vtnet2 configured as opt1 (10.60.1.1/24) and opt2 (10.60.2.1/24) with IP forwarding enabled (net.inet.ip.forwarding=1). A ping from node2 (10.60.1.2, lan-a) to node3 (10.60.2.2, lan-b) succeeds through node1, directly demonstrating routing between the two networks.","evidence_ids":["cmd-10df64927aa74241","cmd-d734b65a7d834030","cmd-046da20d70f045fa"]},{"id":"refuse-telnet","requirement":"Refuse TCP port 23 between lan-a and lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"pfctl -sr shows symmetric 'block return ... quick on vtnet1/vtnet2 ... port = telnet' rules (reject, not silent drop, so it produces RST/refused rather than a timeout) in both directions. A telnet attempt from node2 (lan-a) to node3's address (lan-b) on port 23 returns 'Connection refused', directly confirming the block is enforced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ce42503687384e33","cmd-2c755164510c4ab5"]},{"id":"log-refused","requirement":"Record every refused attempt in a log on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"The fi